WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.420 --> 00:00:05.040
Black Panther's solo big-screen debut came
with an empowering message, a meticulously

00:00:05.040 --> 00:00:08.800
crafted origin story… and not a whole lot
of fan-friendly Easter eggs.

00:00:09.210 --> 00:00:13.240
Wakanda is pretty isolated from the rest of
the Marvel world in every sense, but there

00:00:13.240 --> 00:00:17.360
were still a few details in Black Panther
you may have picked up on... if you're a true

00:00:17.360 --> 00:00:17.860
fan.

00:00:18.140 --> 00:00:18.900
Spoilers ahead!

00:00:19.680 --> 00:00:20.900
Oakland origins

00:00:21.420 --> 00:00:25.670
While a disproportionate amount of the events
within both the MCU and Marvel Comics takes

00:00:25.670 --> 00:00:30.250
place in and around New York City, Black Panther
switches things up a bit and brings audiences

00:00:30.250 --> 00:00:32.720
to Oakland, California during the 1990s.

00:00:33.120 --> 00:00:38.100
This sets up the tragic story of Eric Stevens,
aka Killmonger, whose comic book origins have

00:00:38.120 --> 00:00:38.820
roots in Harlem.

00:00:39.320 --> 00:00:43.340
Switching Harlem to Oakland isn't accidental;
director Ryan Coogler grew up in Oakland,

00:00:43.340 --> 00:00:47.080
and it's also where the real-world Black Panther
Party traces its origins.

00:00:47.300 --> 00:00:51.580
Even though Marvel actively tried to distance
itself from the organization by changing Black

00:00:51.760 --> 00:00:55.640
Panther's name to Black Leopard for a brief
period in the '70s, the politically-charged

00:00:55.640 --> 00:00:57.900
choice of Oakland is undeniable.

00:00:58.380 --> 00:01:01.660
That Public Enemy poster on the wall of N'Jobu's
apartment confirms it.

00:01:02.340 --> 00:01:03.700
Like T'Chaka, like son

00:01:04.620 --> 00:01:09.420
Despite having limited screen time in both
Civil War and Black Panther, T'Challa's father,

00:01:09.420 --> 00:01:11.740
T'Chaka, has a huge influence in the MCU.

00:01:12.229 --> 00:01:16.520
In both films, he's played by award-winning
veteran actor John Kani… except when he

00:01:16.520 --> 00:01:18.940
shows up in the 1990s at N'Jobu's apartment.

00:01:19.270 --> 00:01:23.720
Rather than digitally de-age the elder actor
and end up with a distracting Superman's Mustache

00:01:23.720 --> 00:01:28.760
experience, Marvel hired John Kani's actual
son, Atandwa Kani, to play T'Chaka as a young

00:01:28.760 --> 00:01:29.280
man.

00:01:29.640 --> 00:01:34.360
The younger actor strikes a pretty close image
to his dad, so the casting choice was obvious.

00:01:35.400 --> 00:01:36.160
Many masks

00:01:36.960 --> 00:01:41.159
How do you fit the very important character
of Man-Ape into a movie without relying on

00:01:41.160 --> 00:01:43.500
all the problematic racial baggage that comes
with him?

00:01:43.720 --> 00:01:48.329
For starters, you don't call him Man-Ape,
but use his given name, M'Baku.

00:01:48.329 --> 00:01:51.899
And then you give him a primate mask anyhow,
just in case no one made the connection between

00:01:51.900 --> 00:01:53.640
him and the iconic comic character.

00:01:54.030 --> 00:01:58.240
The hulking warrior from the comics does clash
with T'Challa pretty often, but just like

00:01:58.240 --> 00:02:01.100
in the MCU, they're able to put aside their
differences now and again.

00:02:01.380 --> 00:02:03.960
In the comics, it was for Black Panther's
wedding to Storm.

00:02:04.260 --> 00:02:08.440
As for the horned mask that Killmonger decides
to steal from the museum, it doesn't have

00:02:08.440 --> 00:02:12.880
an exact comic book counterpart, but it does
bear some visual similarities to a mask Killmonger

00:02:12.960 --> 00:02:17.140
dons in ritual combat in 2008's Black Panther
#38.

00:02:17.620 --> 00:02:22.600
But mostly, the MCU's Killmonger is a far
cry from the comics, diverging in both goals

00:02:22.600 --> 00:02:25.640
and backstory, even though both enjoy taking
off their shirts.

00:02:25.909 --> 00:02:30.360
Mercifully, the comic book Killmonger's taste
for beating people with a spiked belt is also

00:02:30.360 --> 00:02:35.400
not on display in the MCU, even if his love
of waterfall-related murders is.

00:02:36.200 --> 00:02:37.000
Remember Vine?

00:02:37.720 --> 00:02:41.810
While shouting a reference to a meme isn't
exactly a "small detail," here's a hint for

00:02:41.810 --> 00:02:43.720
anyone not neck-deep in social media.

00:02:44.140 --> 00:02:48.220
When Shuri questions T'Challa's choice of
footwear by shouting "What are those?", it's

00:02:48.260 --> 00:02:50.840
a reference to the very dankest of memes.

00:02:50.840 --> 00:02:51.660
"Question for you…"

00:02:52.860 --> 00:02:54.700
"What are thoooooose?"

00:02:54.700 --> 00:02:57.300
At least it wasn't "Damn, Daniel", right?

00:02:57.820 --> 00:03:01.860
Even teenagers in the utopia of Wakanda make
very bad choices when it comes to Internet

00:03:01.860 --> 00:03:02.380
usage.

00:03:02.920 --> 00:03:07.060
Bonus shoe joke points go to dropping a reference
to Back to the Future II's iconic self-lacing

00:03:07.080 --> 00:03:10.780
shoes, even though Shuri's version of the
sneakers gets a serious upgrade.

00:03:12.240 --> 00:03:12.760
Neck tech

00:03:13.640 --> 00:03:17.400
Marvel Comics' Black Panther made his on-page
debut kicking the butts of the collective

00:03:17.400 --> 00:03:21.640
Fantastic Four for fun, in nothing more than
a sleek, simple, black outfit.

00:03:22.020 --> 00:03:26.080
As time went on, his comic outfit hasn't really
gone through too many changes, though Black

00:03:26.090 --> 00:03:30.620
Panther would occasionally lose the cape,
and choose different sets of thorny neck adornments.

00:03:30.620 --> 00:03:34.000
Marvel's movies have given us pretty much
the full assortment of Panther's costumes

00:03:34.000 --> 00:03:38.260
already, and when Shuri shows him his different
options for his latest and greatest crime-fighting

00:03:38.260 --> 00:03:40.800
tech, each of those looks comes from a comic
book era.

00:03:41.340 --> 00:03:45.560
Tech (and magic) from the comics also includes
those Kimoyo bead bracelets, which can make

00:03:45.560 --> 00:03:49.340
phone calls, stabilize deadly wounds, and
presumably stream entire seasons of Golden

00:03:49.540 --> 00:03:50.800
Girls without buffering.

00:03:51.020 --> 00:03:54.689
The heart-shaped herb also hails from the
comics, as well as the ancestral plane to

00:03:54.689 --> 00:03:56.460
which it sends those who consume it.

00:03:57.260 --> 00:03:58.320
The Serkis is in town

00:03:59.120 --> 00:04:01.540
Just how many outsiders do get into Wakanda?

00:04:01.960 --> 00:04:05.980
Klaue claims that he's the only non-Wakandan
to have ever seen the country, but that's

00:04:05.980 --> 00:04:06.800
not quite true.

00:04:07.420 --> 00:04:12.040
He's clearly unaware of Marvel’s Avengers:
Infinity War Prelude #1, which shows Shuri

00:04:12.040 --> 00:04:15.590
slowly repairing the one-armed Bucky, both
mentally and physically.

00:04:15.980 --> 00:04:19.740
It won't be long before he gets a replacement
arm, fresh from Wakanda, and gets back into

00:04:19.740 --> 00:04:21.860
fighting shape by the time Thanos arrives.

00:04:22.340 --> 00:04:25.960
The role that Wakanda plays in the battle
against the Mad Titan remains to be seen,

00:04:25.960 --> 00:04:30.120
but if Infinity War's trailers are any evidence,
it's going to be a big one.

00:04:30.700 --> 00:04:31.300
Thanks for watching!

00:04:31.700 --> 00:04:34.000
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:04:34.340 --> 00:04:36.780
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

